A||||8||BroCar 87||Tachometer Drops while I am driving. WHY??||||5||
Z||000000||BroCar 87||10-02-2000||10:42 PM||||I noticed that my tack needle falls while driving. My entire ignitions system is BRAND NEW. Its an 87 4 cylinder with DIS, I don't believe I have a tach filter. DO I? It falls quite a bit, but there is no notice in the driving when it DOES happen. The car drives like it normally does. <BR>My car feels like it does miss at a constant speed though. Maybe this is and AFFECT of the tach falling... I dunno!!<BR>Any suggestions??<BR><P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/NorthFLFieroSIG.jpg">    <BR>87 Coupe "IRON BEAST"<BR>84 SE (project)||||reg||5
Z||000001||JohnnyK||10-02-2000||11:01 PM||||Umm, your not holding the accelerator down?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||BroCar 87||10-02-2000||11:07 PM||||HEY!!!  thats it!<BR>haha, No the tach drops to 0-Zero.<P>I would kick myself in the @$$ if your reply was right, and me for asking such a stupid question!<BR>I think its a little more mechanical than that. THANKS FOR THE HELP THOUGH  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Anyone else?<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/NorthFLFieroSIG.jpg">     <BR>87 Coupe "IRON BEAST"<BR>84 SE (project)||||reg||10
Z||000003||theogre||10-02-2000||11:41 PM||||That I can tell DIS motors don't use a tach filter. If they do it's so well hidden it's invisible.<P>One of the new parts may be bad. I got a bad PIM awhile back. that was fun. Not.<P>May be overheating something. The PIM doesn't like to much heat. Doesn't always like aftermarket (accell) coils either.<P>Something may not have been installed quite right. one of the connectors on the PIM could be loose.<P>I have an article on DIS in my cave.<P>Why did you replace everything?||||reg||1
Z||000004||BroCar 87||10-03-2000||12:49 AM||||theogre, I have been in your cave frequently its a lot of help THANKS! <BR>I replaced everything because I had a bad crank sensor. I went from plugs to wires to coils to module to sensor. I paid the local auto shop to do the sensor and module. I couldn't get it done at my house because I live out on dirt roads. No garage or driveway. (yes constantly washing my car... haha) <BR>Would bad plugs or wires be making it miss at a constant speed?<BR>Also I got a code for my fuel vapor canister a couple weeks ago while sitting too long at a light (in drive). I thought my octane level was too high. Since then I put in lower octane and it hasn't come up again.<BR>BACK to the problem at hand. What tells the tachometer what the right amount of RPM's are?? I remember hooking up my aftermarket tach in my old chrysler laser, and it went to the Coil. <BR>Thanks for the help so far, <BR>Bro outt!!!<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/NorthFLFieroSIG.jpg">      <BR>87 Coupe "IRON BEAST"<BR>84 SE (project)||||reg||4
Z||000005||mrfiero||10-03-2000||01:09 AM||||Bad plug wires on a DIS motor will make it miss/sputter.  I owned an '87 coupe with a low mileage '88 engine in it and it did the same thing.  I pulled my hair out trying to trace the source of the miss and finally replaced the wires and the miss went away.<P>As far as the tach thing.....it likely is in the coil pack....whether it's the PIM or bad coils, who knows?  Check everything out again to be sure.  Something wrong here will also create a miss.<P>Good luck.   ||||reg||1
Z||000006||BroCar 87||10-03-2000||01:56 AM||||Thanks MrFiero. I am going to change out the plugs and wires first. Those are the oldest part of the ignition system and I changed them right before it broke down. So they may be NEW but they MAY be bad also. They were kinda nasty (plugs) before I cleaned em up. <P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/NorthFLFieroSIG.jpg">      <BR>87 Coupe "IRON BEAST"<BR>84 SE (project)||||reg||1
Z||000007||dman||10-03-2000||04:10 AM||||Hello everyone. Hey BroCar 87, I've got an 87 4cyl fiero with DIS that does exactly what you've been describing. I noticed it a couple of months ago but I haven't had the time to check it out. When it does "drop out" I don't feel any difference in engine performance. I suspect the ignition system is running just fine, and that it is the tachometer (or just the signal going to it) that is the problem. Since it usually fixes itself I don't worry to much about it, but if it cuts out longer than a few minutes I'll have to find the time to fix it. If you find the cause, --please-- let me know. Thanks!!!<BR>||||reg||2
Z||000008||theogre||10-03-2000||09:03 AM||||The tach is fed from a terminal on the PIM. I can look it up later.<P>Plugs MUST be gapped at .060. Wrong gap on plugs will cause problems with DIS. Don't waste time with "improved" plugs of any type. DIS likes plain old normal AC or Autolite resistor plugs just fine.<P><BR>what code did you get? there is no feed back to the ECM from the vapor can.<P>Was it 45?||||reg||1
